### The parent tries to face an uncertain future after a serious diagnosis or medical history.

5 of 107 posts.

These posts span a newborn after hypoglycaemic brain injury, a 14-day-old in hospice care, a two-month-old with bilateral optic-nerve colobomas, a four-month-old with hypotonia and poor weight gain, and a medically complex 17-month-old. Parents ask what future abilities or independence might look like, seek outcomes without sugarcoating, or treasure the time they have. Existing responses and gains can coexist with fear, grief and limited predictions.

- "We've seen a developmental psychologist and he's receiving early intervention. ... I'm scared and I fear a future where he won't be able to live a free and independent life which could mean I can't either. I don't know what to do at this point." [sr061-0137]
- "Good outcomes, bad outcomes, somewhere in between — I want to hear all of it. Right now we’re just trying to understand what life could potentially look like for our little girl." [sr086-0103]
- "Well, we just got back from the optometrist appointment where we learned that her optical nerves in both eyes have colobomas which basically cause blind spots in her vision. We don’t have any idea exactly how bad the blind spots are since it’s so early, but we’re scheduled to come back in 6-8 weeks and see how she’s progressed. Understandably, My wife took the news pretty terribly and was crying during the appointment. My mind immediately went to grieving the expected future I was envisioning for my daughter. I asked if this means she won’t be able to drive in the future and the doctor reiterated that it’s too early to tell. She’s still able to track our faces and smile back at us so we know there’s at least some vision." [sr096-0071]
- "If you have any questions please let me know. Our next step is she continues to be low weight and tone is pediatric neurology and I may push for endocrinology and genetics as well depending." [sr104-0099]
- "In just a few hours my little sweetie will be 14 days old. Drs said she wouldn’t make it to term, then she wouldn’t survive birth, then they told us it would only be a few hours. I can’t even imagine when she is pulling the strength from, but I love every single minute I get to spend with her.  I get to sing to her, hold her, and love on her for as long as possible." [sr055-0075]
- "Her little spark gives me so much hope and that is probably the scariest part of the whole thing." [sr055-0077]

**But:** "What makes it hard to wrap my head around is how well she seems to be doing right now. She is moving all 4 arms and legs, tracking things with her eyes, responding to us, smiling some, breathing completely on her own, and today she even started sucking and trying to feed by mouth. She is still being tube fed, but seeing her do these things has given us some hope." [sr086-0098]

