# Digest 20: pattern 12, The child has a known delay or is in therapy

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-20.md. Yes posts: 107. Near posts: 51.

## Pattern 12, share 20: yes posts

### The parent wants to know when delayed speech or understanding will catch up.

9 of 107 posts.

These posts ask when babbling, meaningful words, comprehension or combinations of words will arrive. The children range from about seven-and-a-half months to 24 months; several already gesture, sign or understand well, while one 14-month-old also seems not to understand everyday language. Progress can ease one worry and leave the parent watching the next speech milestone.

- "he has great receptive language, babbles a ton, smiles, engaged, and knows four signs. He gestures (points, claps, waves). But no words. Not even mama or dada. I just feel like I’ll never hear him speak. has anyone been in a similar situation and had everything turn out ok?" [sr018-0020]
- "I have a 14 month old who has a speech delay. She didn’t start babbling until she was almost a year old, and she only just started imitating some sounds. Mainly just “Ah!” Back and forth. No real words, she says mamama but not in a way that she’s referring to me or trying to get my attention. She doesn’t point/wave/clap/etc. I don’t think she really understands anything we say. We use signs for eat, water, milk, and all done. I *think* she understands eat and water because she’ll smile when I say them sometimes, but other than that there is no sign of comprehension. She doesn’t attempt to use any signs. She doesn’t bring things to show me. She doesn’t know come here, stop, hugs/kiss or anything like that. I wouldn’t be as worried about the delay in speech if I thought she actually understood more and was just not talking." [sr037-0074]
- "I feel so discouraged. My daughter has been in EI since 22 months and she’s 24mo now. She signs and can pick up signs pretty fast." [sr070-0042]
- "Anyone else’s baby like this or older child was like this? When did they start? He’s 7.5 months now, will be 8 months late this month." [sr026-0122]
- "I feel like my worries eased up for a moment and now I’m back to worrying about 2 word sentences." [sr078-0084]
- "He’s now in early intervention and on a waitlist for speech therapy. Did your child have a speech delay and if so when did their speech pick up?" [sr126-0024]

**But:** "His language exploded around 16 months and he says more than 50 words, that probably happened in the last month or so." [sr078-0083]

Posts: 1ssti0t 1tcbi5b 1tcgfy1 1tnnh40 1tpj2y4 1uu35iq 1v9firf 1x3oq29 1xe7ltu

### The parent wants more ways to help the child use speech and gestures at home.

5 of 107 posts.

Parents describe modelling words, repeating them, narrating play, reading, signs and pauses, then ask what else they can do. The children are 16 to 23 months old, with one described only within the 18–24-month stage. Some want more intelligible pronunciation or pointing; another wants the particular word “mama,” even while many other words are appearing.

- "I guess I am just looking for more ideas on how to help him. I think he is starting to get more frustrated when I can't read his mind and he can't express what he wants me to do. I feel like I am failing him as a SAHM since spends most of his time primarily with me. Has anyone had a similar story? What helped?" [sr129-0089]
- "They say pointing is an important milestone, but he does not do it yet. I form his hand to point at things or I do it myself while being super expressive. He looks at what I am pointing at up close, but is still trying to understand when I point in the distance." [sr009-0004]
- "They recommend using a mirror to practice her pronunciation. Does anyone have any recommendations of any other techniques that are successful or any other things I can try?" [sr074-0130]
- "But I feel like I really was interested in speech from when he was born, have always talked to him constantly, really try to encourage talking and repeat words to him - we didn’t really learn many new techniques in the speech therapy." [sr097-0050]
- "When he wants me, he’ll shout ‘HEY’ over and over or just come grab my hand. I try to correct him and I speak all the time in third person ‘Mama is giving you XYZ’ etc. but nothing seems to be helping. Has anyone ever dealt with this? Any tips?" [sr093-0056]

**But:** "Now it’s been two months, and he’s saying SO many new words! ‘Ball’, ‘duck’, ‘shoes’, ‘all done’ etc. but NO mama." [sr093-0056]

