### The parent asks what therapy should look like and whether a different approach would help.

4 of 107 posts.

These parents question the amount of direct work, preparation, coaching or visible progress in speech-related sessions. Their children are almost 15 months to almost two years old, and the experience ranges from seven or eight sessions to months of home visits. One was offered specialised skills training rather than speech therapy and wonders whether play is addressing the assessed communication delay.

- "He has completed eight speech therapy sessions (45 minutes each), once a week, but I don't see any progress whatsoever." [sr001-0128]
- "During the sessions, the therapist just shows him toys; I don't see what she is doing building toward anything more substantial. There are days when I want to give up on taking him, but I keep doing it because I'm told that this is supposed to work." [sr001-0131]
- "I was told that since my boy is so young, that in home is the best thing to do. But I feel like the slp doesn’t spend a lot of time working with my child 1:1. It’s mostly observing me and telling me what to do and then tells me to work on her suggestions for the remainder of the week until next session." [sr107-0076]
- "That’s about it, she never has activities set up or appears to have done any prep. I understand a lot of therapy at this age is play based and focused on parent coaching however she only twice has suggested very basic things to me such as “try modeling ‘more’ at home”. She also has not discussed what goals or objectives she has for my daughter. Does this sound typical or should I go with my gut to find a more structured practice?" [sr133-0067]
- "Should I insist on speech therapy, he had his first SST session a couple days ago and she kept mentioning how she isn’t a speech therapist and that all that will happen in the sessions is play. I’m not sure how much my baby will benefit from it if what he needs is help with his speech and communication." [sr116-0066]

**But:** "I understand a lot of therapy at this age is play based and focused on parent coaching however she only twice has suggested very basic things to me such as “try modeling ‘more’ at home”." [sr133-0067]

