### The parent is unsettled by assessment results that differ from what they see or were told.

6 of 107 posts.

Some parents sought help for one concern and received unexpectedly low receptive, cognitive or social-emotional scores. Others find hypotonia and motor-delay labels in the portal after an apparently reassuring four-month checkup, or think a shy 23-month-old did not show normal abilities in the evaluation. The posts span four months to 23 months, with one child recorded in the 18–24-month stage.

- "My 11-month-old was referred to Early Intervention for a gross motor delay, which wound up being classified as mild, but the EI assessors found severe receptive communication delay (4th percentile)." [sr002-0305]
- "I know it's a standardized, validated evaluation but I'm still just so shocked he did so poorly. It seemed like he failed to do so many things that I know he can do in other contexts and he just was not interested or capable of complying with their requests/intent of the activity. It was obvious watching it that he was doing terrible. I just can't believe it's as bad as it is." [sr074-0019]
- "They also mentioned concern about lack of joint attention and I completely agree he demonstrated very little if any for them but that doesn't match with what we see at home. He looks at things we point at, looks back and forth from us to books or the game were playing, loves social games like peek a boo and will some times sort of wait for us to make the next move etc." [sr074-0025]
- "I only now checked the notes in patient gateway about his check up and there are things we never spoke about." [sr085-0053]
- "Now I feel like the report at the end of the week is not going to be representative of what his actual abilities are - not sure what I’m looking for but maybe just reassurance that they’ll adjust as they go?" [sr084-0079]
- "To my surprise, speech wasn’t a flag but his social/emotional part score was that of a 10m old." [sr085-0079]

**But:** "Hes a fun boy, we laugh all day and play a lot but the eye thing is def true. Similarly, he doesn’t play with his friends but goes in a corner and plays on his own." [sr085-0080]

