### The parent struggles to obtain or keep the therapy they think the child needs.

3 of 107 posts.

A parent of a 22-month-old is navigating insurance before the regional centre will pay; another loses early-intervention eligibility after six months despite a remaining walking concern near 18 months. A parent of a four-and-a-half-month-old with oral asymmetry has exhausted money for osteopathy and chiropractic sessions while awaiting insured OT. They ask about next steps, providers, payment or what further intervention might do.

- "The case assistant at the center informed us that they are the "payer of last resort," meaning we must first attempt to secure speech therapy through our insurance." [sr033-0099]
- "LO will be in few days, she was in EI for 6 months because at 11 months she could not even sit up on her own. EI helped a TON, she’s now been crawling for a few months, getting up to stand, cruising etc but not walking yet. She shows no signs of walking. She had her 6 month reassessment this week and was told she does not qualify (they always average the fine and gross motor skills)." [sr108-0082]
- "She has needed a lot of work to get to this point, so I’m really surprised that their opinions were to move forward. PT/OT both say we shouldn’t push her to walk, no need to “rush” walking. Is it really rushing if she’s at 18 months? Since she needed a lot of help learning to crawl, I’m worried she’s going to need similar help with walking - getting her brain to make that connection. I feel like we’ll end up at the same spot again in a few months if she hasn’t made any progress on her own." [sr108-0083]
