# baby-development: pattern 12, The child has a known delay or is in therapy

**Written:** 2026-11-20 from baby-development/evidence/2026-11/second-reading/digest-20.md
**Reviewed by the owner:** not yet

## Limits

These posts come from a keyword-free random sample of 20,000 Reddit posts across twelve parenting subreddits, 1 April–24 October 2026; 2,471 were on-niche. This pattern contains 107 on-niche and 51 near posts: counts are posts tagged by one reader, not people or market prevalence. Forms from different digest readers would be merged by judgement; this single digest’s distinct forms remain separate, and its overlapping tag counts cover any mention, not every example in the claim.

## On-niche posts

### Parents need workable movement and positioning practice at home.

10 of 107 posts.

Exercises and handling can provoke refusal, frustration or distress.

- "She will not let us hold her hands to practice." [sr019-0170]
- "asking for remedial baby handling advice." [sr116-0075]

**But:** "He has made a great deal of progress in other areas" [sr077-0113]

### Parents wait for delayed speech or understanding to catch up.

9 of 107 posts.

Signs or comprehension can coexist with missing words.

- "But no words. Not even mama or dada." [sr018-0020]
- "I don’t think she really understands anything we say." [sr037-0074]

**But:** "His language exploded around 16 months" [sr078-0083]

### Parents notice additional missing skills during existing care.

9 of 107 posts.

New concerns include gestures, hand use, movement and fine-motor play.

- "He was waving but now he hasn’t recently." [sr073-0109]
- "she doesn't do things she should be able to like use a utensil while eating" [sr101-0012]

**But:** "He assured me he thinks my child is fine" [sr121-0008]

### Parents wonder whether delays and other behaviours mean autism.

7 of 107 posts.

Opinions conflict; some fear inherited difficulties, while others welcome assessment.

- "Is arm flapping always an autism sign?" [sr006-0031]
- "I’m terrified it’s in my genetics and I never should have had a baby." [sr062-0054]

**But:** "early services can give him a real advantage" [sr080-0086]

### Parents blame themselves or fear blame for the delay.

7 of 107 posts.

Daily effort does not dispel guilt or family criticism.

- "I am wracked with guilt that I didn’t talk to her “right” or “enough”." [sr074-0088]
- "Now I'm worried I'm going to be blamed for it by my husband and mil" [sr084-0122]

**But:** "is just the happiest, sweetest guy." [sr050-0231]

### Parents question helmet necessity and worry about wearing one.

7 of 107 posts.

They weigh seller incentives, heat, duration and babyhood memories.

- "the company that sells the helmets" [sr078-0089]
- "All memories I make with him at this age will include the helmet." [sr049-0012]

**But:** "We’re willing to do it and pay for it if it’s necessary" [sr078-0090]

### Parents need solids to become a workable source of nutrition.

7 of 107 posts.

Refusal, gagging or poor swallowing persist despite professional help.

- "it’s his only source of nutrition." [sr036-0083]
- "There’s no way she’s even close to being able to consume what she needs with solids" [sr117-0014]

**But:** "now she’ll put a cookie or cracker in her own mouth." [sr117-0013]

### Parents find assessment results hard to reconcile with home life.

6 of 107 posts.

Unexpected scores or unmentioned labels unsettle them.

- "the EI assessors found severe receptive communication delay (4th percentile)." [sr002-0305]
- "not going to be representative of what his actual abilities are" [sr084-0079]

**But:** "the eye thing is def true." [sr085-0080]

### Parents want stories of delayed movement eventually improving.

6 of 107 posts.

Therapy brings uneven progress; two supplied posts repeat one request.

- "She is in physical therapy but it hasn’t helped." [sr070-0089]
- "Has anyone else’s kid done this and when did they walk?" [sr020-0024]

**But:** "We’ve been seeing pt/ot and have seen improvements" [sr024-0011]

### Parents want more ways to encourage speech and gestures.

5 of 107 posts.

They already model language, read or practise pointing.

- "They say pointing is an important milestone, but he does not do it yet." [sr009-0004]
- "I try to correct him" [sr093-0056]

**But:** "he’s saying SO many new words!" [sr093-0056]

### Parents face uncertain futures after serious medical news.

5 of 107 posts.

They seek candid outcomes, grieve expected independence or treasure hospice time.

- "Good outcomes, bad outcomes, somewhere in between — I want to hear all of it." [sr086-0103]
- "I love every single minute I get to spend with her." [sr055-0075]

**But:** "seeing her do these things has given us some hope." [sr086-0098]

### Parents question what speech therapy is accomplishing.

4 of 107 posts.

They want clearer goals, preparation or direct work.

- "but I don't see any progress whatsoever." [sr001-0128]
- "Should I insist on speech therapy" [sr116-0066]

**But:** "I understand a lot of therapy at this age is play based and focused on parent coaching" [sr133-0067]

### Parents want clearer instructions and reassurance about torticollis.

4 of 107 posts.

They question stretching technique, consistency and frightening information.

- "I think I've also been given the wrong leaflet" [sr043-0079]
- "I feel a little lost and am unsure if I’m actually helping." [sr050-0008]

**But:** "Our pediatrician is great and is super positive about everything" [sr063-0044]

### Parents need milk feeding to work despite oral difficulties.

4 of 107 posts.

Work deadlines, weight concerns and sleepy feeding windows constrain life.

- "She’s now latching it seems, but drinks no milk." [sr014-0115]
- "I feel like we can’t leave the house or do anything." [sr096-0085]

**But:** "she can sustain a bigger bottle feeding often taking between 4 and 5 ounces" [sr096-0082]

### Parents struggle to obtain or keep therapy.

3 of 107 posts.

Insurance, eligibility and exhausted funds limit care.

- "we must first attempt to secure speech therapy through our insurance." [sr033-0099]
- "are out of $$ for more sessions." [sr115-0150]

**But:** "EI helped a TON" [sr108-0082]

### Parents find comparisons with other children painful.

3 of 107 posts.

Peers make differences visible despite the child’s strengths.

- "I was really focused on how she wasn't doing as much" [sr002-0207]
- "question my parenting abilities" [sr035-0102]

**But:** "She did warm up eventually and she was climbing around and happy." [sr002-0207]

### Parents want care and friendships to include their child.

3 of 107 posts.

They worry about nursery participation, attention and future belonging.

- "I do worry they’ll group him with the 6 month olds." [sr108-0014]
- "I just want to make sure he has friends in this life." [sr076-0059]

**But:** "daycare doesn't seem to have an issue with it." [sr024-0001]

### Parents ask whether hearing or oral conditions explain delayed speech.

3 of 107 posts.

They question treatment, repeat tests and unnecessary sedation.

- "an ENT to see if she has a tongue tie that is delaying her speech." [sr003-0192]
- "Is there even any point to checking hearing right after a cold?" [sr048-0053]

**But:** "She never had trouble latching." [sr003-0196]

### A parent wants their caregiving role respected.

1 of 107 posts.

Family interference and stigma about DID complicate arranging care after sexual assault.

- "My mother was at this appointment." [sr116-0030]
- "people with DID are completely capable of raising children when there's support in place, which I have." [sr116-0029]

**But:** "My son had a good time playing with the new toys." [sr116-0027]

### Other concerns leave parents seeking explanations or next steps.

4 of 107 posts.

These concern discomfort, head-dropping, enamel defects and a large soft spot; one age is unknown.

- "We have hit a wall" [sr055-0129]
- "they both have missing enamel" [sr099-0118]

**But:** "My son seems to be fine" [sr099-0120]

## Near posts

### Parents need relief from exhausting caregiving.

10 of 51 posts.

Therapy schedules, illness, little help and lost leisure wear them down.

- "Can barely afford the therapy for my youngest." [sr006-0019]
- "I FUCKING HATE MY LIFE." [sr065-0033]

**But:** "my husband tells me don’t worry because we aren’t doing that and I can rest." [sr030-0101]

### Parents grieve expected babyhood or regret another child.

5 of 51 posts.

Appointments, surgery and feeding problems displace enjoyment; accounts include explicit rejection.

- "they've been filled with anxiety and appointments." [sr066-0047]
- "I don’t want her. It was such a mistake to have a second." [sr103-0164]

**But:** "He smiles at me, loves to cuddle, and is honestly wonderful and hitting milestones." [sr066-0046]

### Parents struggle with milk feeds despite trying many changes.

4 of 51 posts.

Small feeds, distress and conflicting opinions leave them searching.

- "it is a full time job" [sr074-0132]
- "Nothing is working." [sr132-0067]

**But:** "She’s otherwise hitting all of her milestones." [sr074-0133]

### Parents struggle to make solids work.

3 of 51 posts.

Refusal or food-holding prompts requests for feeding help.

- "all she’s been wanting to eat is oatmeal for every meal." [sr004-0054]
- "I want to enjoy our time" [sr097-0042]

**But:** "she also was absolutely not concerned about him or his eating" [sr047-0066]

### Caregivers share aids or activities they found helpful.

3 of 51 posts.

Examples include an app, trails and BSL; one professional created materials, and app affiliation is unknown.

- "this one has visual cards and caregiver syncing." [sr053-0165]
- "over time his confidence grew until he eventually spoke a few words." [sr063-0062]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### Caregivers want sleep to work around developmental needs.

3 of 51 posts.

They fear rolling safety, unexplained waking or an unsuitable schedule.

- "how do i keep him safe while i sleep?" [sr005-0003]
- "He’s delayed in speech so he can’t communicate what is going on either" [sr006-0076]

**But:** "those sacks won’t be used anymore" [sr005-0007]

### Parents want family visits and childcare to accommodate needs.

3 of 51 posts.

They describe distress, pool safety and careful feeding.

- "currently that would not be possible without severely stressing him out." [sr056-0038]
- "you have to feed him carefully or he chokes." [sr080-0071]

**But:** "they’re offering to go in 50/50 on a temporary fence" [sr060-0031]

### Parents want adults and institutions to protect their child.

3 of 51 posts.

Concerns include alleged teacher violence, an intimate public image and social-services scrutiny after domestic violence.

- "my sons substitute teacher dumped him out of his chair" [sr010-0146]
- "he’s touching himself while watching a video on YouTube" [sr085-0078]

**But:** "I just didn’t expect them to not be judgemental really so I was pleasantly surprised." [sr092-0075]

### Parents weigh work and family plans around care needs.

3 of 51 posts.

They consider another pregnancy, leaving employment or missing preschool for therapy.

- "I have never been financially dependent on someone in my life" [sr116-0019]
- "Most places near us have a huge waitlist." [sr130-0022]

**But:** "Now we are in calm state , baby picked up and she is an angel." [sr038-0060]

### Parents want friendship and an identity beyond caregiving.

2 of 51 posts.

They want shared hobbies as well as understanding of support needs.

- "there's this hollow loneliness inside of me." [sr006-0084]
- "to be seen as an individual outside of motherhood." [sr035-0030]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### Parents question which therapy approach will suit their child.

2 of 51 posts.

They compare ABA settings or ask how active toddlers participate.

- "Which is more effective, center-based or in-home?" [sr030-0032]
- "What does speech therapy actually consist of?" [sr044-0039]

**But:** "He has no transition issues." [sr030-0033]

### A parent wants to stop pumping but feels guilty.

1 of 51 posts.

Work and pumping take time away from the baby.

- "I feel so guilty wanting to be done with breastfeeding." [sr113-0151]
- "I literally hate pumping." [sr113-0155]

**But:** "Ended up being the best decision and I felt soooooo much better." [sr113-0153]

### A parent seeks candid accounts of possible speech apraxia.

1 of 51 posts.

Diagnosis remains uncertain; the child’s age is unknown.

- "Can take years to confirm I guess." [sr107-0018]
- "Tell me all the good and the bad. What helped and what didn’t?" [sr107-0019]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### A parent wants help encouraging independent walking.

1 of 51 posts.

The child uses support and receives PT; age is unknown.

- "will walk for 10 minutes outside with it." [sr058-0024]
- "Just at a loss on what else to do" [sr058-0025]

**But:** "Nothing wrong physically or neurologically besides his left foot does stick out a little" [sr058-0025]

### A father feels blamed and misrepresented by an evaluator.

1 of 51 posts.

He needs state-funded speech therapy and plans to complain.

- "Dad here just anticipates everything the child wants" [sr044-0131]
- "everything I said was taken as a lie or exaggeration" [sr044-0134]

**But:** "All parents tend to anticipate things! It's completely normal." [sr044-0133]

### A parent wants understanding for imperfect responses to aggression.

1 of 51 posts.

They describe developmental trauma, family violence and occasional restraint; age is unknown.

- "We're not robots?!" [sr038-0092]
- "physically restraining, grabbing and removing from a situation etc." [sr038-0094]

**But:** "We are in a pretty good place now" [sr038-0094]

### A parent wonders whether speech delay and repetition mean autism.

1 of 51 posts.

Low-risk screening has not resolved concerns about a two-year-old.

- "the early intervention assessor wants to re-test in 6 months" [sr132-0103]
- "if we should get an autism assessment." [sr132-0107]

**But:** "speech is improving." [sr132-0102]

### A parent wants to understand apparently contradictory assessment scores.

1 of 51 posts.

They question DAYC-2 labels and its origins; age is unknown.

- "I don't know how to interoperate the results." [sr134-0113]
- "one sales rep bought the right pediatrician a nice lunch." [sr134-0115]

**But:** "I am not in denial of what this all likely means" [sr134-0116]

### Other worries concern teething, a surgical splint and lymph nodes.

3 of 51 posts.

Parents seek alternatives, reassurance or somewhere to recount acute fear.

- "I guess I thought I was doing something wrong." [sr004-0179]
- "his surgical wound is now exposed to the air" [sr033-0028]

**But:** "everything came back reassuring." [sr062-0048]

## Across the on-niche posts

### Parents have already tried home practice and professional treatment.

105 of 107 posts.

Attempts span speech, feeding, movement and extensive treatment combinations.

- "we read daily." [sr129-0087]
- "Tongue/lip tie release, amino acid formula, reflux meds, GI doctor, weekly OT and chiropractor, craniosacral therapy…" [sr055-0127]

**But:** "I stopped after three sessions because the approach felt rough" [sr108-0071]

### Parents receive conflicting advice and sometimes feel undermined.

84 of 107 posts.

Clinicians and relatives influence both care and confidence.

- "We’ve seen two specialists and gotten completely different opinions" [sr044-0016]
- "undermined me, and so on." [sr116-0030]

**But:** "the hospice nurses and doctors are taking amazing care of us." [sr055-0076]

### Parents report paying for therapy, equipment and helmets.

6 of 107 posts.

Spending can stretch budgets without delivering the hoped-for change.

- "Even with copay being $35 for a once a week session, it’s really stretching things." [sr026-0066]
- "I’ve also bought a tummy time pillow" [sr042-0066]

**But:** "my grandma helped me pay for a helmet and now his head is fine" [sr011-0117]

### Parents describe fear, grief and helplessness alongside love.

80 of 107 posts.

They fear difficult futures and mourn expected experiences.

- "I love my little boy so damn much but it wrecks me" [sr026-0063]
- "I’m terrified it’s in my genetics and I never should have had a baby." [sr062-0054]

**But:** "Her little spark gives me so much hope" [sr055-0077]

